Marshaled comprehensive nuclear science framework for detailed explanation

Here is the complete breakdown of every property carried by each nuclide on the Chart of Nuclides:
THE AXES — What positions a nuclide on the chart

Z — Proton number (vertical axis) Defines what element it is chemically. Carbon is always Z=6. Change Z, you have a different element entirely.

N — Neutron number (horizontal axis) Same element, different neutron count = isotope. Carbon-12 and Carbon-14 are both carbon, different N.

A — Mass number A = Z + N. Total nucleons. This is the number after the element name. Co-60, Cs-137, I-131.
THE VALLEY OF STABILITY

The diagonal band of stable nuclides running through the chart. Everything outside this band is unstable and decays. The further from the valley, the faster it decays.

    Above the valley = too many neutrons → decays by Beta-minus
    Below the valley = too few neutrons → decays by Beta-plus or Electron Capture
    Heavy end (Z > 82) = too massive → decays by Alpha

DECAY MODES — What each nuclide does to reach stability

Alpha decay (α) Ejects a helium-4 nucleus (2 protons + 2 neutrons). Loses mass rapidly. Short range in air (~3cm), stopped by paper. High LET — extremely damaging biologically if internalized. Characteristic of heavy nuclides: Ra-226, Rn-222, Po-210, U-238, Pu-239.

Beta-minus decay (β⁻) A neutron converts to a proton inside the nucleus, ejecting an electron and an antineutrino. Penetrates centimeters of tissue. Stopped by plastic or aluminum. Used in therapy: I-131, Lu-177, Y-90, Sr-90.

Beta-plus decay (β⁺) / Positron emission A proton converts to a neutron, ejecting a positron. The positron immediately annihilates with an electron producing two 511 keV gamma photons traveling in exactly opposite directions. This is the basis of PET scanning.

Electron Capture (EC) The nucleus captures one of its own inner orbital electrons, converting a proton to a neutron. Emits characteristic X-rays. Common in heavier neutron-poor nuclides: I-125, Ga-67.

Gamma emission (γ) Not a decay mode by itself — it accompanies other decays when the daughter nucleus is left in an excited state and releases the excess energy as a gamma photon. This is the fingerprint emission — each nuclide emits gamma at specific, fixed energies in keV:

    Cs-137: 662 keV
    Co-60: 1173 keV + 1332 keV
    I-131: 364 keV
    Ra-226: 186 keV
    Tc-99m: 140 keV

Isomeric Transition (IT) A metastable nuclear isomer (designated with an "m") releases energy by gamma emission without changing Z or N. Tc-99m → Tc-99 is the classic example — the entire nuclear medicine industry runs on this. 6 hour half-life, pure 140 keV gamma, no particle emission.

Spontaneous Fission (SF) The nucleus splits into two large fragments plus neutrons. Characteristic of the heaviest nuclides: Cf-252, U-238 (minor branch). Releases enormous energy and multiple neutrons per event.

Neutron emission A free neutron is ejected directly. Occurs in highly neutron-rich nuclides far from stability. Extremely penetrating — requires water or polyethylene to moderate.

Proton emission A free proton is ejected. Occurs in extremely neutron-poor nuclides beyond the proton drip line.

Cluster decay Ejects a nucleus larger than alpha but smaller than fission fragments. Rare, observed in heavy nuclides. Example: Ra-223 emitting C-14.
HALF-LIFE (T½)

The time for half the atoms to decay. This is the single most operationally important property:
Range	Examples
Microseconds	Po-213 (4 µs)
Seconds	Rn-222 daughters
Minutes	Tc-99m precursor Mo-99 decay chain
Hours	Tc-99m (6h), Ga-67 (78h)
Days	I-131 (8d), Lu-177 (6.7d)
Years	Cs-137 (30y), Sr-90 (28y), Co-60 (5.3y)
Thousands of years	C-14 (5,730y), Pu-239 (24,100y)
Billions of years	U-238 (4.47 billion y), Th-232
Stable	Fe-56, Ca-40, Pb-208
DECAY ENERGY (Q-value)

The total energy released per decay event, in MeV. This energy is distributed among the emitted particles. Higher Q = more energetic emissions.

    Qβ⁻ — energy available for beta-minus decay
    Qβ⁺ — energy available for positron emission
    Qα — energy available for alpha decay
    QEC — energy available for electron capture

EMISSION ENERGY (keV/MeV)

Each particle or photon emitted carries a specific energy:

Alpha particles — monoenergetic, discrete peaks. Po-210 emits 5.3 MeV alpha. Dense ionization track.

Beta particles — continuous spectrum from zero to Emax. This is because the energy is shared with an invisible neutrino. The endpoint energy (Emax) is characteristic of the nuclide.

Gamma photons — discrete, fixed energies. This is what a gamma spectrometer measures. Fingerprint identification of the source nuclide is done entirely from these peaks.
LINEAR ENERGY TRANSFER (LET)

How densely the radiation deposits energy per unit path length in tissue (keV/µm):
Radiation	LET	Biological effect
Gamma / X-ray	~0.2 keV/µm	Sparse, low
Beta	~0.2 keV/µm	Sparse, low
Proton	~0.5 keV/µm	Moderate
Neutron	~20 keV/µm	High
Alpha	~80 keV/µm	Extreme — dense ionization

High LET = more biological damage per unit energy deposited. Alpha internalized is catastrophically more damaging than the same energy in gamma.
SPECIFIC ACTIVITY

Radioactivity per unit mass (Bq/g or Ci/g). Short half-life nuclides have enormous specific activity — a tiny microgram is intensely radioactive. Long half-life nuclides have low specific activity — tons of U-238 are only mildly radioactive.
DECAY CHAINS

Many nuclides don't decay directly to stability. They decay through a sequence of daughter products, each with its own half-life and emission type. The uranium decay chain has 14 steps before reaching stable Pb-206. Radon-222 is a decay product of Ra-226, which is itself a product of U-238.

This is critical for your Bosean spike — a spike could represent not just one nuclide but an entire decay chain arriving simultaneously, especially if a gaseous intermediate like Rn-222 has accumulated and then undergone burst decay of its short-lived daughters (Po-218, Pb-214, Bi-214, Po-214 — all within ~30 minutes of each other).
CROSS SECTION (σ)

The probability that a nuclide will interact with an incoming neutron, measured in barns (10⁻²⁴ cm²). Governs neutron activation and fission probability. High cross section nuclides are excellent neutron absorbers (control rods) or fission fuels.
MAGIC NUMBERS

Nuclides with Z or N equal to 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, or 126 are exceptionally stable — filled nuclear shells analogous to noble gas electron shells. These nuclides are the endpoints of many decay chains. Pb-208 has Z=82, N=126 — doubly magic, extraordinarily stable.

That is the complete taxonomy of what separates one radiation source from every other. Each nuclide is essentially a unique combination of all of the above properties simultaneously.

